    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d56">

                <pb facs="0164" n="54" sameAs="frus:frus1931v03/pg54"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/1942</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chinese Ministry of Foreign
                            Affairs at Nanking to the Chinese
                            Legation</hi><anchor n="61" xml:id="d56-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Nanking</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1931-09-23">September 23, 1931</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>With reference to the charge made by Japanese that our soldiers attempted to
                    destroy a bridge of the South Manchurian Railway, the Superintendent’s office of
                    the Peining (Peiping-Mukden) Railway <pb facs="0165" n="55" xml:id="pg55"/>
                    reports that on the night of the 18th Japanese troops started the trouble by
                    blowing up the Liu River bridge of the South Manchurian Railway and afterwards
                    scattered dead bodies of our soldiers nearby so as to fasten the blame upon us
                    for the destruction of the bridge.</p>
                <p>The fact is that before destroying the bridge the Japanese had built a siding
                    around this spot so that there would be no interruption in the running of
                    trains. There was on the bridge a signal tower from which a strict watch was
                    always kept.</p>
                <p>This mode of manufacturing evidence defeats its own ends.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
